From 46b3015daef4aa19fb9f51f0f4f7a2b170a9f377 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The browser Authors <noreply@browser.invalid>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 02:14:20 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 32/61] timer-uptime

Pages no longer see how long the machine has been up. Chrome clamps
DOMHighResTimeStamps (performance.now(), event and animation frame times) as
values of the monotonic clock, which counts from boot, and subtracts two
clamped values, so the rounding of the difference grows with the clock's
size: pages could tell the machine's uptime band, the same for every session
and profile until it reboots, and in principle the boot time itself. The
browser now draws an uptime for the session, 2 to 30 days when it started,
and gives every renderer the offset to its monotonic clock
(--timer-uptime-offset, an internal switch the browser sets on each
renderer's command line); renderers clamp on that clock instead. The values
pages see are unchanged; only their rounding follows the session's clock.
Media stream frames a page reads (a VideoFrame from
MediaStreamTrackProcessor, an AudioData from a microphone track), whose
timestamps are the monotonic clock itself, carry the session's clock too
(core/timing/session_clock_offset.h): a WebRTC loopback no longer reads
the machine's real uptime, and a frame written back to a
VideoTrackGenerator is judged on that clock when its timestamp is taken
as the capture time. Not with --use-chromium-defaults. The switch
declaration rides in switch-resolver.
---
